PRESTON MARKET UPDATE — we expect the proposed development plan to be released later this week.

We followed up last week’s questions at a briefing today.

It turns out that the draft plans align with the ‘Heart of Preston’ proposal submitted to the Standing Advisory Committee by council officers, NOT the version approved by councillors in 2021. That means that the draft plan:

— seeks to migrate the entire site to all-electric over time, with no new gas connections, but does not immediately ban all gas;

— retains all of the market north of Earle Street, but not the south shed;

— allows buildings up to 16 storeys, not 12, albeit with a smaller number of total apartments (as per the video);

— provides 10% affordable housing, not 20%, with the delivery model to be determined closer to the planning permit stage;

— provides 8% of the site as ground-level public open space, with additional elevated green space; and

— includes a potential new kindergarten or childcare centre, but not an MCH centre.

The development plan will be made available to the public through the Engage Victoria website so that everyone has the opportunity to review it and provide feedback directly to the Minister for Planning.

For those interested, the key design teams on the project are Six Degrees Architects, OCULUS, Macroplan, Mosquito Architecture and Sean Godsell Architects.

Very sad news that the Greens MP for Brunswick, Dr Tim Read, passed away yesterday.

Tim was a beloved member of parliament, and the news of his cancer diagnosis earlier this year came as a great shock.

He gave his valedictory speech on 29 July to a packed chamber of every MP from every party.

It was a typically warm and funny speech. He’d needed a blood transfusion and he joked about how the anonymous donors would feel if they knew they had donated to “a politician — and a Green one at that.”

But re-listening to his speech, the really striking thing is the degree to which Tim used that opportunity — a huge outpouring of sympathy for a colleague dying of metastatic melanoma — to do what he had always done: not to talk about himself (he was a very private man in many respects), but to make the case, in a measured and careful way, for specific policy reforms:

— introducing ACT-style limits on rent increases;
— broadening the definition of corruption in the IBAC Act;
— giving IBAC greater investigative powers;
— abolishing group voting tickets;
— retaining VicHealth;
— building more bike lanes and pedestrian crossings; and
— continuing to decarbonise Victoria’s energy system.

Our thoughts are with his family, friends and Greens colleagues.

Thanks to Bicycle Network CEO Alison McCormack for coming in to discuss active transport issues, including:

— 30 kph zones versus protected bike lanes;
— the different roles of different levels of government;
— Darebin’s strategic cycling corridors; and
— Bicycle Network’s campaign for 5% of transport spending to go towards active transport infrastructure.

PRESTON MARKET UPDATE — we understand that local people with planning expertise have seen draft plans for the development.

Apparently, the draft plans meet all requirements of the 2023 planning amendment, all recommendations of the 2023 Standing Advisory Committee report, and all requirements of the original Darebin Council ‘Heart of Preston’ position (!).

The Heart of Preston position included:

— retaining 100% of the existing market footprint;

— allowing apartment buildings of no more than twelve storeys on Murray Road and four storeys on Cramer Street;

— banning all gas use on the site;

— providing 20% affordable housing with a proportion of tenancies weighted towards newly arrived migrants;

— providing at least 10% public open space on site; and

— providing a new council kindergarten and MCH centre, paid for by the market’s private owners.

We are seeking clarification on these points, noting council itself moved away from some of them in 2022.

As per previously, we are also pushing for the public release of any lodged development plan and the public release of the Darebin Council public acquisition feasibility study.

Doorknocking report for Keon Park between Johnson Street and Cheddar Road…

We mainly chatted to people about the new social housing project (see previous post). Other issues people raised included:

— the need for fresh groceries at the Johnson Street shops;

— traffic safety, including rat-running through Arundel Avenue, Linton Street and Lloyd Avenue (where we have advocated for 40 kph limits); whether the Keon Parade speed limit should be reduced from 70 kph to 60 kph; and speeding on Wagga Road;

— pedestrian safety, including at Keon Parade, Cheddar Road, the crossing to JC Donath Reserve, and Johnson Street generally;

— whether the exit from the southern commuter carpark at Keon Park Station should also be an entry;

— rubbish dumping on Wagga Road (we raised this with council, and to their credit, they have installed CCTV);

— the failure of council to enforce local parking restrictions;

— the closure of the counter at Reservoir Police Station;

— the need for toilets at Keon Park Station;

— the condition of Darebin Community Sports Stadium (see our post with Darebin Basketball Association last week);

— the need to trim the melaleucas on Lloyd Avenue;

— the need for better street lighting on Lloyd Avenue;

— possible upgrades for JC Donath Reserve; and

— the question of what is happening to the old kindergarten on Dole Avenue.

We will raise these issues with the relevant state and local decision-makers.

One house on Arundel was basically a shrine to the Gold Coast Suns — not something you see often in Melbourne!

Streets doorknocked: the area enclosed by Johnson Street, Cheddar Road, Keon Parade and JC Donath Reserve.

KEON PARK UPDATE — below are the issues people have raised regarding the Johnson Street social housing development:

— the potential parking implications, including the likelihood of adding to parking pressures around Johnson Street;
— the related issues of council parking enforcement and the difficulty of accessing the northern and southern station carparks from Johnson Street;
— the potential impact on pedestrian safety and traffic congestion, especially at the intersection of Johnson Street and Keon Parade;
— the apartment mix, particularly the proportion of one-bedroom apartments;
— the lack of private apartments or a mixed-tenure approach;
— the liveability of some apartment layouts;
— the adequacy of facilities and spaces for children;
— the adequacy of the communal space;
— the potential community safety and security issues;
— the support services that will be provided and their alignment with resident needs;
— the level of canopy cover and deep soil;
— the resilience to climate change;
— the energy efficiency;
— the need for an all-electric design;
— the need for a local greengrocer or fresh food retailer in Keon Park, and the potential for the ground-floor tenancy to serve that purpose;
— the general scale of the project;
— the design;
— the overlooking and overshadowing of nearby properties;
— the outsourcing of subsidised housing to non-government entities; and
— the importance of developer contributions.

Our understanding of the decision-making process is that Homes Victoria and Hallmarc will consider the feedback and submit their proposal to the Department of Transport and Planning. The community housing arrangements will be finalised with NAHV, Homes Victoria and Housing Australia. While the project is mainly federally funded, the Victorian Minister for Public Transport, the Treasurer and the Minister for Planning will need to provide approvals. 

We will raise the feedback points with all relevant ministers.

