Thank you to Minister Paul Edbrooke MP for visiting Summerhill Residential Park to hear from residents and staff.

Some of the issues discussed included:

— cost-of-living pressures and high site rents relative to the Age Pension;

— the calculation of deferred management fees (noting the current Lifestyle Communities VCAT matter);

— clarity of site agreements, particularly around services, maintenance and community facilities;

— the Allan Labor Government’s recent reforms; and

— the potential for further reforms.

Thank you to everyone who joined us to share their views and insights.

Any development at Preston Market would require two things: approved planning permits and an approved ‘development plan’ — in effect, a blueprint for how the whole site would work.

The development plan would include details of:

— the location and design of any new buildings;
— the protection of the existing market buildings;
— the location of new open space, landscaping and trees;
— the staging of any works; and
— support for traders during any construction.

We have asked at what point such a development plan would be made public.

Electrifying your home helps reduce energy bills and emissions. Thank you to everyone who attended our electrification roundtable with the Minister for Climate Action, Lily D'Ambrosio MP.

Some of the key issues raised were:

— the challenges of high upfront costs for people on pensions and other fixed incomes — and how a room-by-room approach with a focus on efficiency can lower bills over time;

— the importance of enforcement of the new rental standards, including energy efficiency and electrification requirements, that will come into effect in March 2027 (not March 2026, as our newsletter mistakenly said!);

— opportunities to improve the rollout of electrification upgrades in public housing;

— the impact of gas supply charges for households that have mostly electrified but still have one gas appliance;

— the need for fairness in all embedded networks (noting the Victorian Government’s recent announcement that embedded electricity network operators will be required to align their prices with the most competitive rates in the market); and

— how government can better communicate the support that is available.

Thanks to Craig Liddell and the Reservoir Neighbourhood House team for hosting us, and to Louise for making the cakes!

— with Fiona, Neil, Craig, Jenny, Emily, Aleta, Minister D’Ambrosio, Tess, Glenn and Aidyl

Parliament report for last week…

I took the opportunity to chat to ministers about some issues raised during our recent doorknocking, including:

— Gabrielle Williams MP (Minister for Transport Infrastructure) about murals at Reservoir Station, e-scooters on public transport, and naming the playgrounds under the Preston-Bell skyrail;

— Sonya Kilkenny MP (Attorney-General and Minister for Planning) about anti-vilification laws, bail laws, Eric Street, Preston Market, and design standards for townhouses;

— Melissa Horne MP (Roads and Road Safety) about the regulatory framework for pedestrian safety in private carparks like Preston Woolworths; 

— Vicki Ward MP for Eltham (Emergency Services) about support for local firefighters; and

— Ben Carroll MP (Deputy Premier and Minister for Education) about local school issues and Victoria’s great NAPLAN results!

The lower house debated and passed three bills:

— one to update local jobs requirements on government-funded projects;
— one to criminalise posting on social media about certain crimes; and
— one to support the construction of transmission infrastructure for renewable energy.

The upper house also debated and passed three bills relating to WorkSafe, e-scooters, and road and port regulations.

It was a big week in parliament and we’ve posted some other highlights to stories!

Parliament report for last week…

It was one of the busier parliamentary weeks I’ve been involved in, with the final vote taking place at 12:20 am on Thursday night. 

The lower house debated and passed five pieces of legislation:

— a bill to ensure family violence intervention orders operate as intended;

— a bill to introduce compulsory ‘carded play’ for poker machines;

— a bill to reclassify machetes from ‘controlled weapons’ to ‘prohibited weapons’;

— a bill to replace the Fire Services Property Levy with a new emergency services levy; and

— a bill to tighten bail conditions for violent and weapons-related charges.

It’s hard to do justice to those five debates in a short summary, so if you have any questions, just let us know. The bail bill in particular was the subject of some misrepresentative media commentary so very happy to go through the details of that.

Outside the chamber, I also had the opportunity to join some great community events, including a morning tea for World Down Syndrome Day (with Harriet Shing MP and Sophie), an expo for Landcare and ‘Friends of’ environmental groups (with Kate Jost and Luisa Macmillan from Friends of Edwardes Lake and Friends of Merri Creek respectively), and a celebration of St Patrick’s Day (with Eden Foster MP, John Mullahy MP, Gabrielle Williams MP, Iwan Walters MP, Kathleen Matthews-Ward MP and Emma Ní Chuinneagáin).
